PART 1 - THE BUG: both drivers read the same OPEN pickup, then both UPDATE it blindly
  A1 read : SELECT pickup_id FROM pickup WHERE status='OPEN' AND zone_id=1 AND scheduled_date<=CURRENT_DATE ORDER BY requested_at LIMIT 1
        -> [(54105,)]
  B1 read : SELECT pickup_id FROM pickup WHERE status='OPEN' AND zone_id=1 AND scheduled_date<=CURRENT_DATE ORDER BY requested_at LIMIT 1
        -> [(54105,)]
  >>> both sessions picked pickup_id 54105; same row = True
  A2 write: UPDATE pickup SET status='ASSIGNED', shift_id=643 WHERE pickup_id=54105
        -> rowcount = 1
  A3: COMMIT
  B2 write: UPDATE pickup SET status='ASSIGNED', shift_id=644 WHERE pickup_id=54105
        -> rowcount = 1
  B3: COMMIT
  verify  : SELECT pickup_id, status, shift_id FROM pickup WHERE pickup_id=54105
        -> [(54105, 'ASSIGNED', 644)]
  RESULT: both UPDATEs reported success, final shift_id=644 (shift B=644). Driver A's assignment (shift A=643) was silently overwritten = LOST UPDATE.

PART 2 - FIX 1: conditional UPDATE (compare-and-swap: only succeeds if still OPEN)
  A1 read : SELECT pickup_id FROM pickup WHERE status='OPEN' AND zone_id=1 AND scheduled_date<=CURRENT_DATE ORDER BY requested_at LIMIT 1
        -> [(54106,)]
  B1 read : SELECT pickup_id FROM pickup WHERE status='OPEN' AND zone_id=1 AND scheduled_date<=CURRENT_DATE ORDER BY requested_at LIMIT 1
        -> [(54106,)]
  >>> both sessions picked pickup_id 54106
  A2 write: UPDATE pickup SET status='ASSIGNED', shift_id=643 WHERE pickup_id=54106 AND status='OPEN'
        -> rowcount = 1
  A3: COMMIT
  B2 write: UPDATE pickup SET status='ASSIGNED', shift_id=644 WHERE pickup_id=54106 AND status='OPEN'
        -> rowcount = 0
  B3: COMMIT
  verify  : SELECT pickup_id, status, shift_id FROM pickup WHERE pickup_id=54106
        -> [(54106, 'ASSIGNED', 643)]
  RESULT: A updated 1 row, B updated 0 rows; final shift_id=643 (shift A=643). B knows it lost and must pick another pickup.

PART 3 - FIX 2: claim_pickup() uses SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED (work queue)
  A1 claim: SELECT claim_pickup(27, 1)
        -> [(54107,)]
  B1 claim: SELECT claim_pickup(28, 1)
        -> [(54108,)]
  >>> A claimed 54107, B claimed 54108  -> different pickups: True
  A2: COMMIT
  B2: COMMIT
  verify  : SELECT pickup_id, status, shift_id FROM pickup WHERE pickup_id = ANY(ARRAY[54107,54108]) ORDER BY pickup_id
        -> [(54107, 'ASSIGNED', 643), (54108, 'ASSIGNED', 644)]

PART 4 - ISOLATION: READ COMMITTED (default) - the new row appears inside A's transaction
  A1 count: SELECT count(*) FROM pickup WHERE status='OPEN' AND zone_id=1 AND scheduled_date<=CURRENT_DATE
        -> [(82,)]
  B1 insert+commit: INSERT INTO pickup(customer_id, zone_id, scheduled_date, pickup_type, status) VALUES (482, 1, CURRENT_DATE, 'on_demand', 'OPEN') RETURNING pickup_id
        -> [(54110,)]
  B2: COMMIT  (a new OPEN pickup is now committed)
  A2 count: SELECT count(*) FROM pickup WHERE status='OPEN' AND zone_id=1 AND scheduled_date<=CURRENT_DATE
        -> [(83,)]
  A3: COMMIT
  A4 count (new transaction): SELECT count(*) FROM pickup WHERE status='OPEN' AND zone_id=1 AND scheduled_date<=CURRENT_DATE
        -> [(83,)]
  RESULT: inside the transaction A saw 82 then 83; in a new transaction it sees 83.

PART 5 - ISOLATION: REPEATABLE READ - A keeps its frozen snapshot until it commits
  A1 count: SELECT count(*) FROM pickup WHERE status='OPEN' AND zone_id=1 AND scheduled_date<=CURRENT_DATE
        -> [(82,)]
  B1 insert+commit: INSERT INTO pickup(customer_id, zone_id, scheduled_date, pickup_type, status) VALUES (482, 1, CURRENT_DATE, 'on_demand', 'OPEN') RETURNING pickup_id
        -> [(54112,)]
  B2: COMMIT  (a new OPEN pickup is now committed)
  A2 count: SELECT count(*) FROM pickup WHERE status='OPEN' AND zone_id=1 AND scheduled_date<=CURRENT_DATE
        -> [(82,)]
  A3: COMMIT
  A4 count (new transaction): SELECT count(*) FROM pickup WHERE status='OPEN' AND zone_id=1 AND scheduled_date<=CURRENT_DATE
        -> [(83,)]
  RESULT: inside the transaction A saw 82 then 82; in a new transaction it sees 83.

PART 6 - DEADLOCK: A locks p1 then wants p2; B locks p2 then wants p1
  A1 lock p1: SELECT pickup_id FROM pickup WHERE pickup_id=54113 FOR UPDATE
        -> [(54113,)]
  B1 lock p2: SELECT pickup_id FROM pickup WHERE pickup_id=54114 FOR UPDATE
        -> [(54114,)]
  A2: A asks for p2=54114 (held by B) -> blocks
  B2: B asks for p1=54113 (held by A) -> circular wait; PostgreSQL's detector fires after ~1 s
  session A: DEADLOCK DETECTED -> deadlock detected
  session B: got the second lock
  RESULT: exactly one session was chosen as the victim and rolled back; the other proceeded.

PART 6b - THE FIX: both sessions lock in the SAME order (lowest pickup_id first) -> no deadlock
  A1 lock p1: SELECT pickup_id FROM pickup WHERE pickup_id=54113 FOR UPDATE
        -> [(54113,)]
  B1: B also asks for p1=54113 first (waits for A)
  A2 lock p2: SELECT pickup_id FROM pickup WHERE pickup_id=54114 FOR UPDATE
        -> [(54114,)]
  A3: COMMIT (releases p1 and p2)
  session B: finished, no deadlock

